{%- comment -%}
  Was `platformos_filters | concat: standard_filters`, a concatenation of two independently
  maintained lists. That published `map` and `split` TWICE — the platform reopens both, so each
  name appeared once from each list, and the standard entry carried no parameters at all. Nothing
  broke only because the two happened to agree on the return type; a consumer looking a filter up
  by name got whichever the sort happened to put first.

  There is one list now. The platform publishes the WHOLE vocabulary in `data.filters`: its own
  filters from `platform_filters.rb` and Liquid's own from `docs/liquid/standardfilters.rb`, both
  documented as annotations in the application repository and serialized together by the YARD
  generator, which is also where a name the platform reopens is resolved to the platform's
  definition. So there is nothing left to concatenate here, and no name can appear twice.

  `api/liquid/core_filters` and `api/liquid/standard_filters` are what this used to assemble, and are
  deleted: the prose they carried now lives with the annotations, in the same place as the list of
  filters, so a filter can no longer be dropped here by forgetting to describe it.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- comment -%}
  OUTPUT, not `return`: the page invokes this partial with `{% render %}`, which renders its output
  and discards any returned value — only `{% function %}` captures a `return`. Written as a `return`,
  this published an EMPTY array while every piece below it was correct, which is the worst shape a
  defect here can take: `filters.json` is how a consumer decides whether a filter exists, so an empty
  one reports every filter in every file as unknown and blocks the write.
{%- endcomment -%}
{% liquid
  function filters = 'api/liquid/platformos_filters', data: data
%}
{{ filters | array_sort_by: 'name' }}
